//? Title: Approximate Counting: Morris's Algorithm
//? Subtitle: Unbiasedness and the exact variance by a one-line recurrence, the exact distribution, and pixel counts of handwritten digits in five-bit registers with datamunge's algorithms::MorrisCounter
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Counting to \(n\) exactly takes \(\log_2 n\) bits. Morris (1978) showed how
to count in about \(\log_2\log_2 n\) bits. Store an exponent \(c\), and
raise it with probability \(a^{-c}\) at each event. We derive, from a
one-step recurrence, that the estimate \((a^c-1)/(a-1)\) is unbiased with
variance exactly \((a-1)\,n(n-1)/2\). We check both against the exact
distribution of \(c\), computed by dynamic programming. The data is the
stream of 115 008 pixels in `dm::datasets::digits()`, counted by intensity
in 17 counters. The busiest value, intensity 0, occurs 56 272 times. That
needs a 16-bit exact counter, while the base-2 Morris counter fits in 5
bits. Its relative error is large, with a theoretical standard deviation
of 71%. Moving the base towards 1 buys accuracy cheaply: each halving of
\(a-1\) halves the variance for about one more bit. Averaging independent
counters is far more expensive. At the same total of 10 bits, one base-1.01
counter has an error 7.1% while two base-2 counters have 50%.
)

\toc

> The data: a stream of pixels

The digits data holds 1 797 images of \(8\times8\) pixels, each an
intensity from 0 to 16. Read row by row, they form a stream of 115 008
events, and we want to know how often each intensity occurs. That calls for
17 counters, which is the setting Morris had in mind: many counters, each
in a small register, where an approximate count is good enough.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<long> count(17, 0);
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r)
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) ++count[static_cast<int>(digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r))];
  std::print("{} pixels in {} images.\n\n| intensity | count | bits for an exact counter |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n",
             64 * digits.nrows(), digits.nrows());
  for (int v = 0; v < 17; ++v)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", v, count[v], static_cast<int>(std::floor(std::log2(count[v]))) + 1);
}
```
