Intensity 0, the background, dominates with 56 272 pixels, and full ink
(16) is next with 10 456. The other values occur 2 500 to 4 300 times each.
Exact counters need 12 to 16 bits.

> The method

>> The update

A Morris counter with base \(a>1\) keeps an integer exponent \(c\),
starting at 0. On each event it sets \(c\gets c+1\) with probability
\(a^{-c}\), and otherwise leaves it alone. The estimate of the number of
events is

$$
\hat n=\frac{a^{c}-1}{a-1}\qquad(\hat n=2^c-1\text{ for }a=2).
$$
\alttext(n hat equals a to the c minus 1, over a minus 1; for a equal 2 this is 2 to the c minus 1.)

The first event always raises \(c\) to 1. After that, raising \(c\) from
\(k\) to \(k+1\) takes on average \(a^k\) events. The exponent therefore
grows like \(\log_a n\) and needs only \(\log_2\log_a n\) bits.

```
MORRIS(a):
    c = 0
    on each event:  with probability a^(-c):  c = c + 1
    estimate:       (a^c - 1) / (a - 1)
```

>> Unbiasedness in one line

Let \(X_n=a^{c_n}\) after \(n\) events. Conditioning on the current \(c\),

$$
\mathbb{E}[a^{c'}\mid c]=a^{c}\,(1-a^{-c})+a^{c+1}\,a^{-c}=a^{c}+(a-1).
$$
\alttext(The expected value of a to the c prime given c equals a to the c times 1 minus a to the minus c, plus a to the c plus 1 times a to the minus c, which equals a to the c plus a minus 1.)

So each event adds exactly \(a-1\) to \(\mathbb{E}[X]\) on average, and
\(\mathbb{E}[X_n]=1+n(a-1)\). Equivalently, \(X_n-n(a-1)\) is a martingale.
Hence \(\mathbb{E}[\hat n]=\mathbb{E}[(X_n-1)/(a-1)]=n\): the estimate is
unbiased for every \(n\) and every base.

>> The exact variance

The second moment satisfies the same kind of recurrence:

$$
\mathbb{E}[a^{2c'}\mid c]=a^{2c}(1-a^{-c})+a^{2c+2}a^{-c}=a^{2c}+(a^2-1)\,a^{c}.
$$
\alttext(The expected value of a to the 2 c prime given c equals a to the 2c plus a squared minus 1 times a to the c.)

Taking expectations and summing from \(X_0^2=1\),

$$
\mathbb{E}[X_n^2]=1+(a^2-1)\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\big(1+k(a-1)\big)
=1+(a^2-1)\Big(n+(a-1)\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\Big).
$$
\alttext(The expected value of X n squared equals 1 plus a squared minus 1 times the sum over k from 0 to n minus 1 of 1 plus k times a minus 1, which equals 1 plus a squared minus 1 times n plus a minus 1 times n times n minus 1 over 2.)

Subtracting \((1+n(a-1))^2\) and simplifying gives
\(\operatorname{Var}X_n=(a-1)^3n(n-1)/2\), so

$$
\operatorname{Var}(\hat n)=\frac{(a-1)\,n(n-1)}{2},\qquad
\frac{\operatorname{sd}(\hat n)}{n}\to\sqrt{\frac{a-1}{2}} .
$$
\alttext(The variance of n hat is a minus 1 times n times n minus 1, over 2, and the relative standard deviation tends to the square root of a minus 1 over 2.)

For \(a=2\) the relative standard deviation tends to \(1/\sqrt2\approx71\%\).
It does not shrink as \(n\) grows. Two remedies follow from the formula:

- averaging \(m\) independent counters divides the variance by \(m\);
- moving the base towards 1 makes the variance proportional to \(a-1\).

>> Counting many events at once

Feeding the counter one coin flip per event costs \(O(n)\) time even though
the state changes only about \(\log_a n\) times. At level \(c\) the number
of events until the next increment is geometric with success probability
\(p=a^{-c}\). It can be drawn directly as
\(\lceil\ln U/\ln(1-p)\rceil\) for \(U\) uniform on \((0,1]\).
`MorrisCounter::add(n, rng)` does this, which makes it practical to
simulate counts in the millions many times over. The exponent it produces
has exactly the same distribution as \(n\) calls to `increment`.

> Counting the digits in small registers

Each intensity gets one counter. The stream is fed one pixel at a time,
in three runs with bases 2, 1.1 and 1.01.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/approximate_counting_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<int> stream;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r)
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) stream.push_back(static_cast<int>(digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r)));
  std::vector<double> exact(17, 0.0);
  for (int v : stream) ++exact[v];

  const std::vector<std::pair<double, dm::RGB>> bases = {
      {2.0, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}}, {1.1, dm::RGB{245, 158, 11}}, {1.01, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}}};
  std::vector<std::vector<alg::MorrisCounter>> counters;
  std::mt19937_64 rng(2024);
  for (const auto& [a, colour] : bases) {
    counters.emplace_back(17, alg::MorrisCounter(a));
    for (int v : stream) counters.back()[v].increment(rng);
  }

  std::print("| intensity | exact | base 2 | base 1.1 | base 1.01 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int v = 0; v < 17; ++v)
    std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} (c={}) | {:.0f} (c={}) | {:.0f} (c={}) |\n", v, exact[v],
               counters[0][v].estimate(), counters[0][v].exponent(), counters[1][v].estimate(),
               counters[1][v].exponent(), counters[2][v].estimate(), counters[2][v].exponent());
  std::print("\n| base | register bits (largest counter) | theoretical relative sd | observed RMS relative error |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (std::size_t b = 0; b < bases.size(); ++b) {
    const double a = bases[b].first;
    int bits = 0;
    double sq = 0;
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    for (int v = 0; v < 17; ++v) {
      bits = std::max(bits, counters[b][v].register_bits());
      const double rel = counters[b][v].estimate() / exact[v] - 1;
      sq += rel * rel;
      x.push_back(v + (static_cast<double>(b) - 1) * 0.2);
      y.push_back(100 * rel);
    }
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.1f}% | {:.1f}% |\n", a, bits, 100 * std::sqrt((a - 1) / 2), 100 * std::sqrt(sq / 17));
    plot.points(x, y, std::format("base {}", a), bases[b].second, 5.0);
    plot.abline_h(100 * std::sqrt((a - 1) / 2), bases[b].second, 1.0)
        .abline_h(-100 * std::sqrt((a - 1) / 2), bases[b].second, 1.0);
  }
  plot.abline_h(0, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
      .title("Morris estimates of the digit pixel counts")
      .x_label("intensity")
      .y_label("relative error (%)")
      .size(900, 520)
      .save_svg("build/approximate_counting_digits.svg");
}
```
