| 1.01 | 10 | 7.1% | 7.7% |
\image(figures/approximate_counting_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Relative error of each of the 17 counters for three bases. The horizontal lines of each colour mark \(\pm\sqrt{(a-1)/2}\), one theoretical standard deviation.)
\alttext(Dot plot of relative error in percent against intensity 0 to 16. Red base-2 points scatter widely, from about minus 86 to plus 212 percent, with most between red lines at plus and minus 71 percent. Amber base-1.1 points lie mostly within amber lines at plus and minus 22 percent, the largest at plus 66. Blue base-1.01 points hug zero, between about minus 15 and plus 18 percent, around blue lines at plus and minus 7 percent.)

The base-2 counters do what the theory says, which is not much. The 56 272
background pixels end at \(c=15\) and an estimate of 32 767, 42% low. The
2 627 pixels of intensity 7 are estimated at 8 191, more than three times
too many. One step of \(c\) doubles or halves a base-2 estimate, so errors
come in factors of two. The whole set of counters needs only 4 bits each,
since no exponent passed 15. Base 1.1 needs 7 bits and has a typical error
near 20%. Base 1.01 needs 10 bits, still well below the 12 to 16 bits of
exact counters, and its RMS error is 7.7%. In each case the observed RMS
relative error over the 17 counters is close to the theoretical
\(\sqrt{(a-1)/2}\). Seventeen counters is a small sample, so close
agreement is all that can be expected.

> Checking the theory exactly

The variance formula is easy to check exactly. `morris_exponent_pmf`
propagates the distribution of \(c\) through all \(n\) events by dynamic
programming: at each event, mass at level \(k\) moves to \(k+1\) with
probability \(a^{-k}\). We do this for the background count
\(n=56\,272\) at base 2. From the distribution we compute the mean and
variance of \(\hat n\) and compare them with the formulas. We also compare
the distribution with 20 000 simulated counters that use the fast `add`.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/approximate_counting_pmf.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::uint64_t n = 56272;
  std::print("| base | quantity | exact distribution | closed form | 20 000 simulations |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> levels, exact_p, sim_p;
  for (double a : {2.0, 1.1}) {
    const auto pmf = alg::morris_exponent_pmf(n, a, a == 2.0 ? 40 : 200);
    double mean = 0, sq = 0;
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < pmf.size(); ++c) {
      const double est = (std::pow(a, static_cast<double>(c)) - 1) / (a - 1);
      mean += pmf[c] * est;
      sq += pmf[c] * est * est;
    }
    std::mt19937_64 rng(99);
    std::vector<double> sims;
    std::vector<double> freq(pmf.size(), 0.0);
    for (int r = 0; r < 20000; ++r) {
      alg::MorrisCounter mc(a);
      mc.add(n, rng);
      sims.push_back(mc.estimate());
      freq[static_cast<std::size_t>(mc.exponent())] += 1.0 / 20000;
    }
    const double sm = std::accumulate(sims.begin(), sims.end(), 0.0) / sims.size();
    double sv = 0;
    for (double s : sims) sv += (s - sm) * (s - sm);
    sv /= sims.size() - 1;
    std::print("| {} | mean of estimate | {:.1f} | {} | {:.1f} |\n", a, mean, n, sm);
    std::print("| {} | sd of estimate | {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} |\n", a, std::sqrt(sq - mean * mean),
               std::sqrt(alg::morris_variance(n, a)), std::sqrt(sv));
    if (a == 2.0)
      for (std::size_t c = 10; c <= 22; ++c) {
        levels.push_back(c);
        exact_p.push_back(pmf[c]);
        sim_p.push_back(freq[c]);
      }
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .bars(levels, exact_p, "exact", dm::RGB{96, 165, 250})
      .points(levels, sim_p, "simulated", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 5.0)
      .title("Exponent after 56 272 events, base 2")
      .x_label("exponent c")
      .y_label("probability")
      .size(860, 480)
      .save_svg("build/approximate_counting_pmf.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| base | quantity | exact distribution | closed form | 20 000 simulations |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | mean of estimate | 56272.0 | 56272 | 56248.1 |
| 2 | sd of estimate | 39790.0 | 39790.0 | 39268.8 |
| 1.1 | mean of estimate | 56272.0 | 56272 | 56413.4 |
| 1.1 | sd of estimate | 12582.7 | 12582.7 | 12732.4 |
\image(figures/approximate_counting_pmf.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Distribution of the base-2 exponent after 56 272 events: exact probabilities from dynamic programming (bars) and frequencies in 20 000 simulated counters using the geometric skip (red points).)
\alttext(Bar chart of probability against exponent from 10 to 22. Almost all the mass is on 14 to 17: about 0.10 at 14, 0.40 at 15, 0.37 at 16 and 0.11 at 17, with about 0.01 at 18. Red simulation points sit on top of the bars.)

The mean and standard deviation from the exact distribution equal the
closed forms to the printed precision: 56 272 and 39 790 for base 2, and
12 582.7 for base 1.1. The simulations agree within their sampling error.
The estimate's distribution is heavily skewed, so 20 000 replicates pin down
its standard deviation only to about a percent. The exact exponent is
almost always 14 to 17, so the base-2 estimate is almost always one of
16 383, 32 767, 65 535 or 131 071. Unbiasedness comes from rare large
values balancing frequent small ones, not from estimates near the truth.

> Spending bits: smaller base or more counters?

Both remedies cost memory. A counter that must reach \(n\) needs
\(\lceil\log_2(c_{\max}+1)\rceil\) bits, where we take \(c_{\max}\) as the
99.9% quantile of the exponent from the exact distribution. One counter
with base \(a\) has relative standard deviation \(\sqrt{(a-1)(n-1)/2n}\).
Averaging \(m\) base-2 counters costs \(m\) registers and divides the
standard deviation by \(\sqrt m\). The block tabulates both for
\(n=56\,272\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/approximate_counting_bits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::uint64_t n = 56272;
  auto bits_for = [&](double a) {
    const auto pmf = alg::morris_exponent_pmf(n, a, 2000);
    double cum = 0;
    std::size_t c = 0;
    while (c + 1 < pmf.size() && cum + pmf[c] < 0.999) cum += pmf[c++];
    return static_cast<int>(std::floor(std::log2(c))) + 1;
  };
  const int b2 = bits_for(2.0);
  std::print("| base a | bits | relative sd | | base-2 counters averaged | bits | relative sd |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> xb, yb, xm, ym;
  const std::vector<double> bases = {2.0, 1.5, 1.2, 1.1, 1.05, 1.02, 1.01, 1.005};
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < bases.size(); ++i) {
    const double a = bases[i];
    const int bits = bits_for(a);
    const double sd = std::sqrt(alg::morris_variance(n, a)) / n;
    const int m = 1 << i;
    const double sdm = std::sqrt(alg::morris_variance(n, 2.0) / m) / n;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.1f}% | | {} | {} | {:.1f}% |\n", a, bits, 100 * sd, m, m * b2, 100 * sdm);
    xb.push_back(std::log2(bits)); yb.push_back(std::log10(100 * sd));
    xm.push_back(std::log2(m * b2)); ym.push_back(std::log10(100 * sdm));
  }
  std::print("\nAn exact counter for {} needs {} bits.\n", n, static_cast<int>(std::floor(std::log2(n))) + 1);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(xb, yb, "smaller base", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .points(xb, yb, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5.0)
      .lines(xm, ym, "averaging base 2", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0)
      .points(xm, ym, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 5.0)
      .abline_v(4, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
      .title("Accuracy bought per bit, n = 56 272")
      .x_label("log2 total bits")
      .y_label("log10 relative sd (%)")
      .size(860, 500)
      .save_svg("build/approximate_counting_bits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| base a | bits | relative sd | | base-2 counters averaged | bits | relative sd |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | 5 | 70.7% | | 1 | 5 | 70.7% |
| 1.5 | 5 | 50.0% | | 2 | 10 | 50.0% |
| 1.2 | 6 | 31.6% | | 4 | 20 | 35.4% |
| 1.1 | 7 | 22.4% | | 8 | 40 | 25.0% |
| 1.05 | 8 | 15.8% | | 16 | 80 | 17.7% |
| 1.02 | 9 | 10.0% | | 32 | 160 | 12.5% |
| 1.01 | 10 | 7.1% | | 64 | 320 | 8.8% |
| 1.005 | 11 | 5.0% | | 128 | 640 | 6.2% |

An exact counter for 56272 needs 16 bits.
\image(figures/approximate_counting_bits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Relative standard deviation against total memory for one counter with a smaller base (blue) and for averages of base-2 counters (red). The vertical line marks the 16 bits of an exact counter. Each point in the red series doubles the number of counters.)
\alttext(Two curves of log10 relative standard deviation against log2 of total bits. The blue curve falls steeply from about 1.85 at 5 bits, log2 about 2.3, to 0.7 at 11 bits, log2 about 3.5. The red curve starts at the same point but falls slowly, reaching about 0.8 only at 640 bits, log2 about 9.3. A vertical line at log2 equal 4, 16 bits, marks the exact counter.)

A smaller base wins by a wide margin. Each halving of \(a-1\) halves the
variance and costs about one bit, because \(c_{\max}\approx\log_a n\) and
\(\log_2\log_a n=\log_2\ln n-\log_2\ln a\), with \(\ln a\approx a-1\).
Averaging must double the number of registers to halve the variance. At 10
bits, one base-1.01 counter achieves 7.1% while two base-2 counters
achieve 50%. Even at base 1.005 the counter needs 11 bits, still fewer
than the 16 of an exact counter. Averaging is useful when the counters
must be updated independently, as in sketches and distributed systems. It
is not an efficient way to buy accuracy.

> Summary

- A Morris counter raises its exponent with probability \(a^{-c}\). Since
  \(\mathbb{E}[a^{c'}\mid c]=a^c+(a-1)\), the estimate \((a^c-1)/(a-1)\) is
  unbiased, and the same recurrence on \(a^{2c}\) gives the exact variance
  \((a-1)n(n-1)/2\).
- The exact distribution from `morris_exponent_pmf` and 20 000 simulations
  with the geometric skip in `MorrisCounter::add` both agree with the
  formulas.
- On the digit pixel stream base-2 counters fit in 4 or 5 bits instead of
  12 to 16, with errors of tens of percent and occasionally a factor of
  three. Base 1.01 brings the RMS error down to 7.7% in 10 bits.
- To buy accuracy, shrink the base rather than average counters. Halving
  \(a-1\) costs about one bit, while halving the variance by averaging
  doubles the memory.

```{cpp, eval="no"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

std::mt19937_64 rng(1);
alg::MorrisCounter counter(1.01);   // base a: relative sd ~ sqrt((a - 1) / 2)
counter.increment(rng);             // one event
counter.add(1'000'000, rng);        // a million events in O(log n) time
double n_hat = counter.estimate();  // (a^c - 1) / (a - 1), unbiased
int    bits  = counter.register_bits();
double var   = alg::morris_variance(1e6, 1.01);
```
